// =============================================================================
// FoodOra — Prisma schema (Phase D4)
//
// Multi-file schema (Prisma >= 6.7, GA). The folder is wired up in
// `database/package.json` -> "prisma": { "schema": "./prisma/schema" }.
//
// One file per bounded context, mirroring `backend/src/modules/*` so a module
// stays independently maintainable (Phase D1 rule).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONVENTIONS — applied to every model in this schema
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. IDs are application-generated, prefixed, lexicographically sortable
//    strings ("ven_01J8…", "usr_01J8…"). Prisma has no `@default` that can
//    produce a prefix, so `IdService.next('ven')` in `common/ids` mints them
//    and every `create` passes one. This preserves the ids the frontend and its
//    seed data already use (`ven_*`, `usr_*`, `cat_*`, `cus_*`, `off_*`,
//    `cpn_*`, `cpk_*`, `cao_*`, `rid_*`, `job_*`, `dzn_*`, `bpol_*`, `mpl_*`,
//    `qrc_*`, `tst_*`, `wtx_*`, `addr_*`, `doc_*`, `post_*`) — deep links
//    minted during Phase C keep resolving after the cutover.
//    Pure join tables use composite primary keys instead.
//
// 2. AUDIT FIELDS. Every persisted row carries `createdAt`, `updatedAt`,
//    `deletedAt`, plus `createdBy` / `updatedBy` / `deletedBy` on anything a
//    human edits. `types/common.ts::BaseEntity` already exposes the first
//    three to the frontend, so the read models need no reshaping.
//
// 3. SOFT DELETE. `deletedAt IS NULL` means active. Enforced globally by a
//    Prisma client extension (`infrastructure/prisma/soft-delete.extension.ts`)
//    that rewrites `findMany`/`findFirst`/`count`/`aggregate` and turns
//    `delete` into an update. Immutable financial records (payments, ledger
//    entries, order events, dispatches) deliberately have NO `deletedAt` —
//    they are never deleted, only superseded.
//
// 4. OPTIMISTIC LOCKING. Mutable aggregates carry `version Int @default(0)`.
//    Writes go through `updateMany({ where: { id, version }, data: { …,
//    version: { increment: 1 } } })`; a 0-row result raises `ConflictError`
//    (GraphQL `CONFLICT`). Append-only tables have no `version`.
//
// 5. MONEY is `Decimal @db.Decimal(14, 2)` — exact base-unit amounts, never
//    float. Rates/fractions are `Decimal @db.Decimal(6, 4)` (0.0875 = 8.75%).
//    Resolvers convert to `number` at the GraphQL boundary because the
//    frontend types money as a plain `number` in the entity's currency.
//    See D2 §Money for why Decimal beat minor-unit integers here.
//
// 6. ENUM LABELS are stored in the frontend's kebab-case vocabulary via
//    `@map`, so a Postgres row reads identically to the TypeScript union
//    ("cloud-kitchen", "rider-assigned", "free-delivery"). D5 §Enums explains
//    how those reach the wire unchanged.
//
// 7. SNAPSHOTS. Where the frontend embeds an immutable copy of another entity
//    (`Order.vendor`, `Order.address`, `Reservation.venue`,
//    `Subscription.plan`, `CateringQuote.service`, `OrderLifecycle.rider`) the
//    snapshot is stored as `Json` **and** the live FK is kept beside it. The
//    JSON serves the read model with zero reshaping; the FK serves joins,
//    analytics and referential integrity.
//
// 8. DERIVED STATE IS NEVER STORED. The Phase C convention holds: no
//    `isExpired`, no `isUsed`, no reservation-slot table, no review aggregate,
//    no subscription calendar. Those are computed fields in the resolver layer
//    (D5 §Derived fields). The only exceptions are the denormalised counters
//    the frontend already reads (`Vendor.rating`/`reviewCount`,
//    `Offer.claimed`) — maintained transactionally, see `RatingAggregate`.
// =============================================================================

generator client {
  provider        = "prisma-client-js"
  output          = "../../../backend/src/infrastructure/prisma/generated"
  previewFeatures = ["postgresqlExtensions", "relationJoins", "views"]
}

datasource db {
  provider   = "postgresql"
  url        = env("DATABASE_URL")
  directUrl  = env("DATABASE_DIRECT_URL")
  extensions = [pg_trgm, unaccent, citext, btree_gin]
}
